---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import CatStage from "../components/CatStage.astro";
import AttentionTrace from "../components/AttentionTrace.astro";
import DownloadCta from "../components/DownloadCta.astro";
import { softwareApplicationSchema, websiteSchema } from "../lib/site";

const title = "Look Me — Smart Blink & Eye Break Reminder";
const description = "A private desktop companion that notices long screen sessions and reminds you to blink, look away, and stand up—without uploading camera footage.";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/"
  alternates={[
    { hreflang: "en", path: "/" },
    { hreflang: "zh-Hans", path: "/zh-cn/" },
    { hreflang: "x-default", path: "/" },
  ]}
  schemas={[websiteSchema, softwareApplicationSchema]}
>
  <SiteHeader currentPath="/" />

  <main id="main-content">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="shell hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">PRIVATE BY DESIGN · MACOS, WINDOWS & LINUX</p>
          <h1>A reminder that <span class="soft-line">waits for the right moment.</span></h1>
          <p>Look Me is a tiny desktop companion that notices when a screen session runs long, then nudges you to blink, look into the distance, or stand up. No account. No camera upload. No noisy timer guessing when you are there.</p>
          <div class="hero-actions">
            <a class="button" href="/download/">Download free</a>
            <a class="button button-secondary" href="/how-it-works/">See how it works</a>
          </div>
          <p class="hero-note">Detection and 30-day activity history stay on this device.</p>
        </div>
        <CatStage />
      </div>
      <div class="shell"><AttentionTrace /></div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="why-heading">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">WHY CONTEXT MATTERS</p>
            <h2 id="why-heading">A timer knows the clock. Look Me notices the session.</h2>
          </div>
          <p>Fixed reminders arrive during calls, after you have already stepped away, or while the app cannot see you. Look Me pauses when sensing is unavailable and responds to observable habits instead.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="proof-grid">
          <article class="proof-card">
            <span>01 / BLINK</span>
            <h3>Waits for a complete blink</h3>
            <p>It looks for an open–close–open sequence across both eyes before recording a blink. A long gap can trigger a gentle two-blink prompt.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="proof-card">
            <span>02 / DISTANCE</span>
            <h3>Counts observed screen time</h3>
            <p>The 20-minute distance-break rhythm advances while a usable face signal is present, rather than treating an empty chair as screen time.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="proof-card">
            <span>03 / PRESENCE</span>
            <h3>Stops when you are away</h3>
            <p>Lock the screen, leave the configured schedule, or pause monitoring and the camera is released. Hiding the widget does not silently change collection behavior.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-tint" id="features" aria-labelledby="features-heading">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">ONE QUIET COMPANION</p>
            <h2 id="features-heading">Three habits, one readable rhythm.</h2>
          </div>
          <p>Each reminder can be switched off independently. The companion stays out of the way until the corresponding behavior calls for it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-rail">
          <article class="feature-row">
            <span class="feature-time">AFTER A LONG GAP</span>
            <h3>Blink with the cat twice</h3>
            <p>When enabled, a prolonged gap between detected blinks invites two slow blinks. Completing them dismisses the prompt; a cooldown prevents repeated nudges.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="feature-row">
            <span class="feature-time">EVERY 20 MINUTES</span>
            <h3>Look beyond the screen</h3>
            <p>A calm 20-second horizon appears after 20 minutes of observed screen time. Skip it when needed, or use the free browser timer before installing.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="feature-row">
            <span class="feature-time">YOUR INTERVAL</span>
            <h3>Stand up on your schedule</h3>
            <p>Choose a sedentary interval from 1 to 600 minutes. Look Me distinguishes a completed stand-up movement from a brief tracking loss.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="feature-row">
            <span class="feature-time">LAST 30 DAYS</span>
            <h3>Review a local activity timeline</h3>
            <p>Explore blink, screen, posture, and yawn events by day. The timeline lives in IndexedDB on your computer and automatically drops records older than 30 days.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="rule-heading">
      <div class="shell horizon-panel">
        <img src="/assets/horizon-break.webp" width="1400" height="420" loading="lazy" alt="A soft illustration of distant hills and a lake at sunrise" />
        <div class="horizon-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">20 · 20 · 20</p>
          <h2 id="rule-heading">Give near focus a pause.</h2>
          <p>The American Optometric Association recommends looking at something 20 feet away for 20 seconds after every 20 minutes of screen use to help ease digital eye strain.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/tools/20-20-20-timer/">Start the free browser timer<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-dark" id="privacy" aria-labelledby="privacy-heading">
      <div class="shell privacy-layout">
        <div class="content-block">
          <p class="eyebrow">LOCAL MEANS LOCAL</p>
          <h2 id="privacy-heading">The camera can help without becoming a cloud service.</h2>
          <p>MediaPipe analyzes frames in memory on your computer. Look Me keeps small behavior events—not photos, video, or face coordinates—and requires no account.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="privacy-ledger">
          <div class="ledger-row"><span>Processed</span><strong>Camera frames, in memory, on this device</strong></div>
          <div class="ledger-row"><span>Stored</span><strong>Six event types and paired screen/posture intervals</strong></div>
          <div class="ledger-row"><span>Never stored</span><strong>Photos, video, raw landmarks, or screen contents</strong></div>
          <div class="ledger-row"><span>Retention</span><strong>Thirty days, removed automatically</strong></div>
          <div class="ledger-row"><span>Account</span><strong>None</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="difference-heading">
      <div class="shell privacy-layout">
        <div class="quote-panel">
          <blockquote>“A reminder is useful only if you have not learned to ignore it.”</blockquote>
          <p>Look Me uses a visible companion, responsive timing, and explicit controls so the reminder feels connected to what is happening—not like another productivity alarm.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="content-block">
          <p class="eyebrow">BUILT TO BE KEPT ON</p>
          <h2 id="difference-heading">A companion, not a compliance dashboard.</h2>
          <p>There are no streaks, health scores, accounts, or leaderboards. The cat mirrors a detected blink, yawns occasionally, and offers a small amount of life between reminders.</p>
          <p style="margin-top:20px"><a class="text-link" href="/blink-reminder/">Explore the blink reminder<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-tint" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <div><p class="eyebrow">BEFORE YOU INSTALL</p><h2 id="faq-heading">The honest answers.</h2></div>
          <p>Look Me supports screen habits. It is not a medical device and cannot tell you why your eyes feel uncomfortable.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details>
            <summary>Does Look Me diagnose or treat dry eye?</summary>
            <p>No. It can remind you to blink and take breaks, but it cannot diagnose dry eye disease, measure tear quality, or replace an eye examination. Read the <a href="/dry-eyes-from-screen/">dry eyes and screen use guide</a>; persistent pain, redness, light sensitivity, discharge, or sudden vision change needs professional care.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Does it record me or capture my screen?</summary>
            <p>No. Camera frames are processed in memory and discarded. The app does not capture screen content. Its local timeline stores small behavior events such as a detected blink or the start and end of an observed screen interval.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What happens when the camera cannot see me?</summary>
            <p>Blink and posture sensing pause, and the app explains that sensing is unavailable. It does not replace the missing signal with a hidden timer.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Can every reminder be turned off?</summary>
            <p>Yes. Blink, distance, and sedentary reminders have independent switches, plus a master monitoring switch and configurable monitoring windows.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Is the app signed?</summary>
            <p>Not yet. Current builds are free preview releases and may trigger macOS Gatekeeper or Windows SmartScreen warnings. The download page explains the limitation before you install.</p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <DownloadCta />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
